# Development and Testing

## Code Generation

### Generating the SDK

The interface that this project provides, including what CLI commands are available and what configuration is required is defined in {{ if isHostedOnGitHub .Specification }}[`{{ .ManifestFileName }}`]({{ linkToManifest .Specification .ManifestFileName }}){{ else }}`{{ .ManifestFileName }}`{{ end }}. From this file the Go SDK code is generated using {{ if .Specification.Public }}`go-sdk-generator`{{ else }}the [Go SDK Generator](https://github.com/ARM-software/go-sdk-generator){{ end }}. Follow these steps to make changes to the interface.

#### Prerequisites

##### On Linux

Install `go-sdk-generator` using whichever method your environment supports:

1. If your distribution package repositories already provide the tool, install it with the system package manager such as `apt`, `dnf`, or `yum`.
2. Otherwise, install it from the project's published release artefacts.

##### Other systems

- Install the SDK Generator from its release page.

#### Steps

1. Manually change the interface definition in {{ if isHostedOnGitHub .Specification }}[`{{ .ManifestFileName }}`]({{ linkToManifest .Specification .ManifestFileName }}){{ else }}`{{ .ManifestFileName }}`{{ end }}.
2. Run the Go SDK Generator

```
go-sdk-generator generate -i {{ .ManifestFileName }} -o ./ -g {{ .TargetedGoVersion }}
```

> :book: Note: The `-g` flag specifies the targeted Go version in semver format (e.g., `1.25.0`). Use the Go version from your project's `go.mod` file.

> :book: Note: Adding `--overwrite` will regenerate all the files including `go.mod` and files containing some state.

### Generating Mocks

The tests use [https://github.com/uber-go/mock](GoMock). Mocks are generated by `mockgen` for the interfaces defined in this project.

If you add a new interface file and want mocks to be generated for it you will need to add a comment to the file. This comment indicates that the command specified should run whenever `go generate ./...` is run at the top level of the go project. See [the Go documentation on generation](https://go.dev/blog/generate).

For example, to run mockgen, add this comment:
```
//go:generate go tool mockgen -destination=mocks/mock_$GOPACKAGE.go -package=mocks <package namespace>/$GOPACKAGE IMyInterface
```
This assumes the `go version` used is greater than 1.24.

You can then generate all mocks by running:
```
go generate ./...
```

### Precommit

A [pre-commit](https://pre-commit.com/) hook configuration is provided to enforce some tasks on `git commit`.
Run `pre-commit install` to install pre-commit into your git hooks. pre-commit will now run on every commit.

If you want to manually run all pre-commit hooks on a repository without creating hooks, run `pre-commit run --all-files`.

To run individual hooks use `pre-commit run <hook_id>`.

# GitHub Secrets Configuration

> :information_source: **For CI/CD only**. Local builds normally use the credentials already available in your development environment.

Some workflows may require repository secrets when they need to fetch private dependencies, download private release assets, or create automated pull requests.

## When secrets are needed

1. Review the workflow files under `.github/workflows/` to see which secrets, if any, are expected.
2. Add those secrets only where needed:
   - **Actions:** Settings -> Secrets and variables -> Actions
   - **Dependabot:** Settings -> Secrets and variables -> Dependabot
3. Use least-privilege credentials with only the scopes required for the workflow.

If the repository and all of its dependencies are public, no extra secret configuration may be required.

# Dependency upgrades

For dependency upgrades, Dependabot is used and news files are auto-generated to document the change.
If your upgrade workflows require access to private resources, configure the necessary secrets before enabling that automation.

# Releasing

## Release Types

The CI supports three release flows:

- `development` for snapshot releases
- `release` for stable releases
- `beta` for pre-releases


| Type        | Purpose              | Version Number Format                          | GitHub Release | News Files Deleted |
| ----------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- | :------------: | :----------------: |
| Release     | General Availability | `<minor>.<major>.<patch>`                      |      Yes       |        Yes         |
| Beta        | Integration Testing  | `<minor>.<major>.<patch>-beta.<commit number>` |      Yes       |         No         |
| Development | Development Testing  | `<minor>.<major>.<patch>-dev+<git hash>`       |       No       |         No         |

> :warning: releases can be made from any branches but
> it is recommended that they are only made from the `main` branch.

### Release workflow

1. Navigate to the GitHub Actions page for this repository.
2. Select the **Run Workflow** button and type which kind of release you would like to make (i.e. release, beta or development).

### Version Numbers

The version number will be automatically calculated, based on the news files.

{{ if .Specification.TagLatest -}}
Stable releases also maintain a `latest` tag. It lets consumers follow published releases without updating references, but the moving tag is not reproducible and may introduce breaking changes; pin a version where stability matters.

